,95,109,97,100,95,99,111,110,116,101,120,116,95,98,117,100,103,101,116,46,112,121,96,32,97,110,100,32,96,104,95,109,97,100,95,114,101,115,117,109,101,95,100,101,99,105,115,105,111,110,46,112,121,96,46,32,83,116,100,108,105,98,45,111,110,108,121,46,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],"stderr":[],"exit_code":0,"match_count":27,"file_matches":[{"path":"/Users/kimhawk/.agents/skills/h-mad/SKILL.md","matches":[{"line_number":3,"content":"description: \"Orchestrate the seven-phase H-MAD development workflow end to end with project invariants, independent audits, test-first implementation, verification, and closure. Use for /h-mad feature, /h-mad do feature, /h-mad status, or /h-mad reset feature.\""},{"line_number":28,"content":"| `/h-mad status [<feature>]` | Auto-bootstrap if needed. Read-only. Print state from `docs/.bkit-memory.json`. Surface stale `phase = \"step5\"` flags (heuristic: `autonomous_entry_ts > 60min` ago AND `halt_reason = null`). |"},{"line_number":66,"content":"— **except under `/h-mad status`, which is documented read-only and must stay so.** Item 3 writes"},{"line_number":68,"content":"widens that verb's blast radius into the user's home config. Under `status`, report item 3 as unmet"},{"line_number":297,"content":"| `cannot_judge` | The state file EXISTS and could not be read — truncated mid-write, invalid JSON, unreadable. **Stop. Do NOT initialize and do NOT resume.** This is the absence of evidence, not evidence of absence: the store may hold dozens of records and a live owner for this very feature. Inspect the file by hand, restore it from `git`/backup if it is corrupt, and only then re-run the oracle. It is deliberately not `start_fresh` — that token would have you create a record over a feature another session is working. **Second cause:** a declared or unknown non-Claude host (`HMAD_HOST` set to anything other than empty or `claude`) called without `--session-id` — the oracle cannot check ownership, so it refuses to route; pass the session id your adapter's §\"Context budget and claims\" names, do not repair the file. |"},{"line_number":374,"content":"  coexisting plugins. A FAIL therefore cannot be detected by exit status, and"},{"line_number":489,"content":"- **5f** — **Derive the 5c sha, never `git merge-base`.** `python3 ~/.claude/skills/h-mad/scripts/h_mad_baseline_sha.py --branch <feature-branch> [--trunk main]`, reading the `BASELINE:` token, never `$?`. 5c is `git checkout -b …; commit impl-plan + audit files`, so the 5c commit is the branch's FIRST commit and `git merge-base main <branch>` returns the commit it forked FROM — off by exactly the impl-plan commit, and off plausibly, since both are real shas on the right branch. Measured (J41): merge-base `b5c8f41` against real 5c `730cc16`, 313 lines apart — the impl-plan and its three audits, which the wrong base feeds into the review as newly-added content when they are a separate INPUT to it. `UNVERIFIED` is a verdict, not an error: the branch's first commit does not touch an impl-plan, so something was committed before 5c and the rule does not hold — find the commit by hand, and note the token reports it as `candidate=` rather than `sha=` precisely so an unvouched value cannot be  [... truncated (1031 chars total)]"},{"line_number":494,"content":"  **Run the mutations through `h_mad_mutation_harness.py` rather than by hand.** `python3 ~/.claude/skills/h-mad/scripts/h_mad_mutation_harness.py <spec.json>`, where the spec names the suite command and each mutation as an exact `find`/`replace`. Read the `MUTATION:` token, never `$?`. The harness exists because the hard part of this pass is not choosing mutations, it is proving each one **landed**: an anchor that matches zero times mutates nothing, the suite stays green, and the run reports the guard as enforced — the failure `invariants.base.md` §\"Mutation verification\" names, and one every hand-rolled harness has to re-derive. It refuses any anchor not found exactly once — printing `hint:` lines with the near-miss line numbers, so a drifted anchor no longer costs a manual re-grep — restores the tree on every path including an interrupt, and re-runs the suite afterwards to prove the restore landed. The mutation run performs the sibling sweep itself before the baseline command and re [... truncated (1031 chars total)]"},{"line_number":513,"content":"  `codex-authorship`. Record `codex_status=exhausted` or `unavailable` with"},{"line_number":514,"content":"  `h_mad_state_write.py --feature <feat> --set codex_status=exhausted <state_file>`,"},{"line_number":652,"content":"  status line, or to decide whether to keep waiting. Then one poll per 30–60s is right,"},{"line_number":748,"content":"   running `sleep 2; exit 9` reported `{\"satisfied\":true,\"status\":\"exited\",\"exitCode\":0}`."},{"line_number":845,"content":"2. **`git status` / `git diff`** — enumerate what actually landed. Artifacts present with"},{"line_number":993,"content":"- **6a-prime** — architectural review via agy (`references/agy-architectural-reviewer-prompt.md`). Inputs: Phase 5 diff (BASE = 5c sha; HEAD = 5g sha) + audited design. **Derive the 5c sha, never `git merge-base`.** `python3 ~/.claude/skills/h-mad/scripts/h_mad_baseline_sha.py --branch <feature-branch> [--trunk main]`, reading the `BASELINE:` token, never `$?`. 5c is `git checkout -b …; commit impl-plan + audit files`, so the 5c commit is the branch's FIRST commit and `git merge-base main <branch>` returns the commit it forked FROM — off by exactly the impl-plan commit, and off plausibly, since both are real shas on the right branch. Measured (J41): merge-base `b5c8f41` against real 5c `730cc16`, 313 lines apart — the impl-plan and its three audits, which the wrong base feeds into the review as newly-added content when they are a separate INPUT to it. `UNVERIFIED` is a verdict, not an error: the branch's first commit does not touch an impl-plan, so something was committed before 5c and [... truncated (1031 chars total)]"},{"line_number":2577,"content":"reads; it does not write. `git status --short` before and after a cycle should be identical, and a"},{"line_number":2597,"content":"It prints `ASSEMBLE: PASS <path> <size> sentinel=<s> size_status=verified|unverified` or"},{"line_number":2610,"content":"**Read `size_status=` on the same line, not just the token.** `verified` means the prompt"},{"line_number":2612,"content":"`references/agent-substrate.md`). `size_status=unverified` still dispatches, because"},{"line_number":2728,"content":"   **Never gate an audit on the dispatch's `result.status`.** The report file and its `.done` marker are the delivery contract; `status` is not. ANY failed or refused tool call yields `status: ERROR` beside a complete, correct answer, and the cause is usually incidental to the audit — measured three times with three unrelated causes: a refused `.tmp` write (`grounding-evidence-coverage` impl-plan cycle 22 pass B, on a report carrying two independently-verified real findings), a `find_by_name` timeout plus a `view_file` on a nonexistent path (2026-08-24, 31 tool calls / 29 ok, schema-correct report), and a `write_to_file` rejected for a missing argument that the agent immediately retried successfully (2026-08-24). This is the same rule `h_mad_review_evidence.py` follows for 6a-prime (step 6a-prime above): report `result.status`, never gate on it. Read the file and the marker."},{"line_number":2851,"content":"If `codex_status` records Codex unavailable and `fallback_agent=grok`, prefer"},{"line_number":2865,"content":"`codex_status` declaration:"},{"line_number":2869,"content":"  --feature <feature> --set codex_status=exhausted        # available|unavailable|exhausted"},{"line_number":2951,"content":"  TDD gate hook (`hooks/h-mad-tdd-gate.sh`) reads the **same** `codex_status` for whichever feature"},{"line_number":2992,"content":"- `h_mad_pane_janitor.py` — post-probe pane cleanup: `snapshot` / `plan` / `clean` + CLI printing `JANITOR: PLANNED|CLEANED|NOTHING|SNAPSHOT candidates=N settled=J closed=K skipped=S` or `JANITOR: REFUSED reason=<r>`, exit 0 on a verdict / 2 on a refusal. **Dry run unless `--apply`.** Refusal reasons: `cannot_identify_self`, `baseline_unreadable`, `baseline_malformed`, `too_many_candidates`, `orca_unavailable`, `orca_unparseable`, `orca_error`. The hard part is not closing panes, it is deciding which panes are the probe's: `worker-start` panes inherit the worktree name as their title, so they are indistinguishable from the operator's own agent pane, and each of the seven hand-runs re-typed the keep-list from memory. So take a `snapshot --worktree <path> --out <f>` BEFORE the probe and the keep-list is recorded rather than remembered; a candidate is then a pane in that worktree, absent from the baseline, and not the caller's own — the caller's handle is re-read live from `orca terminal  [... truncated (1031 chars total)]"},{"line_number":3051,"content":"- `h_mad_review_evidence.py` — 6a-prime evidence gate: `scan()` + CLI printing `EVIDENCE: PASS|NONE tools=N ok=K failed=J thinking=T [status=…]`, exit 0 on a verdict / 2 on `EVIDENCE: UNREADABLE reason=no_log|empty_log|unsupported_format`, which carries **no counts** so a cannot-judge cannot read as a zero. Answers only \"did the review read anything\" — never whether its findings are right. Counts any tool reaching `DONE`, knows no tool names, and reports `result.status` without gating on it. `thinking=` sums `usage.thinking_tokens` over completed `agent_response` steps and is reported for the same reason `status` is — triage, never a verdict (J49). Stdlib-only."},{"line_number":3052,"content":"- `h_mad_context_budget.py` — orchestrator context budget in two modes. `--mode run` prices the RUN against an 80% ceiling, printing `CTXBUDGET: OK|HALT mode=run used=N window=N pct=P ceiling=80` — no `projected=`, because a run cap forwards nothing (§\"Run-context ceiling\"). `--mode advisor` is the default and its output is unchanged, because `hooks/h-mad-advisor-warn.sh` parses it live; the verdict words differ (`HALT` vs `DENY`) so no consumer can confuse a dying run with an over-budget advisor call. Advisor mode: `last_context_tokens()` + CLI printing `CTXBUDGET: OK|DENY used=N window=N pct=P projected=N ceiling=C`, exit 0 on a verdict / 2 on `CTXBUDGET: UNKNOWN reason=…` (no transcript, no usage record yet, bad window) — which carries **no `used=`** so a cannot-judge can never be read as an `OK`. Prices an `advisor()` call before you make it (§\"Orchestrator context hygiene\"). Reads the newest **non-sidechain** assistant turn's `input + cache_creation + cache_read`: summing across t [... truncated (1031 chars total)]"},{"line_number":3053,"content":"- `h_mad_host.py` — host classifier: `classify_host()` reads `HMAD_HOST` and returns `claude` (unset, empty or `claude`), `declared` (`codex`, `agy` or `grok`) or `unknown` (any other value; no strip, no case fold). No CLI. Read by `h_mad_context_budget.py` and `h_mad_resume_decision.py`. Stdlib-only."}]}]},"locations":[]}
